<article class="env-remark" id="049E">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/049E">Remark <span data-tag="049E">76.3.5</span>.</a> Let $X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces. For some applications (of radicial morphisms) it is enough to require that for every $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \to Y$ where $K$ is a field </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
the space $|\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \times _ Y X|$ is a singleton, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there exists a monomorphism $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(L) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \times _ Y X$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$K \subset L$ is purely inseparable. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> If needed later we will may call such a morphism <em>weakly radicial</em>. For example if $X \to Y$ is a surjective weakly radicial morphism then $X(k) \to Y(k)$ is surjective for every algebraically closed field $k$. Note that the base change $X_{\overline{\mathbf{Q}}} \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\overline{\mathbf{Q}})$ of the morphism in Example <a href="/tag/0483" data-tag="0483">76.3.3</a> is weakly radicial, but not radicial. The analogue of Lemma <a href="/tag/0484" data-tag="0484">76.3.4</a> is that if $X \to Y$ has property ($\beta $) and is universally injective, then it is weakly radicial (proof omitted). </p>
</article>